Theorem 6.3 is stated in a manner that is independent of dimension - that is, so that it applies to functions f:X ⊆ R'' -> R for any n >= 2. 
In the case n = 2, there is another geometric interpretation of Theorem 6.3: Suppose you are mountain climbing on the surface z = f(x, y). 
Think of the value of f as the height of the mountain above (or below) sea level. 
If you are equipped with a map and compass (which supply information in the xy-plan only), then if you are at the pointon a mountain with xy-coordinates (map coordinates) (a, b), Theorem 6.3 says that you should move in the direction parallel to the gradient ∇f(a, b) in order to climb the mountan most rapidly. 
